+//! The block compressor: what turns a column block into the bytes a segment file carries.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments`. Columns are how the *representation* gets small — a series
+//! written once and counted, a clock written as its steps, a flat line written as one run — and this
+//! is the second half of the same claim: the bytes that remain are still full of the repetition a
+//! telemetry block has, and a general-purpose compressor finds what the encodings above did not.
+//! The two are not alternatives. `zstd` at its default level compresses a block that has already
+//! been delta-encoded and run-length-encoded far better than it compresses the rows it came from,
+//! and it is the reason a segment file is smaller than the sum of its columns.
+//!
+//! ```text
+//!  in:   the column block, `encode::column_block`
+//!  out:  one `zstd` frame, at [`LEVEL`]
+//! ```
+//!
+//! Two things this module deliberately does **not** promise, because both are the point of the gate
+//! that judges it:
+//!
+//! * **the compressed bytes are not a format.** `zstd` may answer different bytes for the same input
+//!   after a release, so a segment is judged by the round trip and by its size and never by a pinned
+//!   compressed vector — a course is not allowed to fail a learner for a dependency's choice;
+//! * **a smaller file is not the claim on its own.** [`LEVEL`] is a fixed number rather than a knob
+//!   to be tuned until the budget passes: a level a deployment could raise is a trade between time
+//!   and bytes, and `lakelet/4.16-profiling` is where a decision like that gets measured.
+//!
+//! # Why the decompressor is given a limit
+//!
+//! Whoever decompresses a file is choosing how much memory to spend on the file's behalf, and a
+//! compressed block can describe far more output than it costs to store. So the call takes the
+//! **length the segment header promised** as its limit, and a frame that would decompress to more
+//! than that is a refusal: a damaged or hostile file gets to fail a check instead of choosing this
+//! process's allocation.
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+/// The `zstd` level every segment is compressed at.
+///
+/// Chosen once, here, because it is a number a reader of the format needs to be able to find: a
+/// deployment that raised it would write segments this build still reads — the frame says how it was
+/// compressed — but the *pages* about the format would be describing a build nobody runs.
+pub const LEVEL: i32 = 3;
+
+/// Compress one column block into the bytes a segment file carries.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Store`] when the compressor itself fails — which for a block that fits in memory means
+/// the machine refused an allocation, not that the records were unusual.
+pub fn compress(block: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error> {
+    let _ = block;
+    todo!("step lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments")
+}
+
+/// Decompress one block, refusing to produce more than `limit` bytes.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Protocol`] when the bytes are not a `zstd` frame, when the frame is cut short, and when
+/// it decompresses to more than `limit` — the three ways a file that someone else wrote can be
+/// something other than what the segment around it claims.
+pub fn decompress(frame: &[u8], limit: usize) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error> {
+    let _ = (frame, limit);
+    todo!("step lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments")
+}
