+//! Reading a column block back into the records it holds.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments`. [`records`] is the inverse of
+//! [`crate::engine::segment::encode::column_block`], and it is the half of the format that decides
+//! what a *stranger's bytes* do. The rule it obeys is `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery`'s, applied to a
+//! closed artefact: there is no prefix to fall back on, so a block is either the whole layout
+//! `4.7` writes or a refusal that names what was wrong — never a panic, and never the records it
+//! happened to get through before the bytes ran out.
+//!
+//! Three things are checked before anything is trusted, because each one is how a reader turns a
+//! corrupt file into a corrupt *answer*:
+//!
+//! * **the tag.** A column whose tag is not one [`crate::engine::segment::encode`] writes is refused
+//!   by number, rather than decoded as whatever its payload looks like;
+//! * **the lengths.** Every length in the block is compared with the bytes actually there before it
+//!   is used as an end, an offset or a capacity: a block that states a payload longer than it is, a
+//!   count no bytes can satisfy, or a run longer than the records left is a refusal. Nothing here
+//!   can reserve what a damaged field claims, which is what keeps a corrupt file from becoming an
+//!   allocation;
+//! * **the agreement between the columns.** A series column that runs to 900 records, a timestamp
+//!   column of 1000 and a value column of 4 is not three answers, it is one damaged block: the
+//!   count in the block's first eight bytes is what every column has to add up to, and a column
+//!   that adds up to anything else is refused.
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+use crate::protocol::batch::Record;
+
+/// The records a column block holds, in the order they were encoded.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Protocol`] when `block` is not the layout
+/// [`crate::engine::segment::encode::column_block`] writes — the sentence names which column and
+/// which part of it disagreed, because the reader of a failing case in the field is a person with a
+/// hex dump and no debugger.
+pub fn records(block: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<Record>, Error> {
+    let _ = block;
+    todo!("step lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments")
+}
