+//! A segment: the closed file a flush writes, with its records laid out in **columns**.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments`. `lakelet/4.5-batch-writes` made one call write a group of
+//! records and counted what it cost; what it did not change is what those records *look like* once
+//! they are on the device. So far they are rows: every record carries its own series name, its own
+//! timestamp and its own eight-byte value, and a telemetry series pays that at every sample. This
+//! module is the other layout — the series in one stream, the timestamps in another, the values in a
+//! third, each encoded against the redundancy it actually has — and `docs/format.md` is where that
+//! layout is written down, because a format nobody else can read is a bug with a deadline.
+//!
+//! ```text
+//!  0..4         `MAGIC`: the four bytes that say this file is a lakelet segment
+//!  4            `VERSION`: the layout's version, one byte
+//!  5            `COLUMNS`: how many column streams follow, one byte — this build writes 3
+//!  6..10        u32, little endian: the **column block**'s length, before compression
+//!  10..         one sealed block, in the shape `wal::segment` gives a record:
+//!                 10..14      u32, little endian: the compressed length (c)
+//!                 14..14+c    the compressed column block: one `zstd` frame
+//!                 14+c..18+c  u32, little endian: the seal over the two above
+//! ```
+//!
+//! The column order is **fixed**, and it is the order a query wants it in: series, timestamp,
+//! value. A reader that needs one column still walks the framing of the ones before it, which is
+//! the price of writing every column's length in front of it — and the reason
+//! `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` will be able to say which columns it read.
+//!
+//! Three choices in that layout are worth their reasons, because each one is a thing a file format
+//! can get wrong:
+//!
+//! * **the file declares itself.** [`MAGIC`], [`VERSION`] and [`COLUMNS`] are what make "this is not
+//!   this build's segment" a *decision* instead of a guess — the discipline
+//!   `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery` brought to the log, applied to a closed artefact. And a segment is
+//!   either whole or refused: a log is replayed to a prefix, a segment has no prefix to offer,
+//!   because nothing is being appended to it;
+//! * **every byte is checked.** The column block travels as one sealed blob
+//!   (`wal::checksum`), and the header's own fields are each compared with the bytes around them: a
+//!   byte that flipped, a file that was cut short and a file that is somebody else's are three
+//!   refusals, and none of them is a shorter answer;
+//! * **the block states its uncompressed length.** A reader of a compressed blob has to know how
+//!   much memory it is being asked for *before* it asks, so the length is in the header and the
+//!   decompressor is handed it as a limit: a file cannot choose this process's allocation, and a
+//!   byte that flipped there is refused by the length comparison after the block decompresses.
+//!
+//! What this module deliberately does not decide: how many records one segment holds, or when a new
+//! one starts (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`), and when bytes reach the device
+//! (`lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr` owns that for the log; a segment is written whole, because half a
+//! segment is not a segment).
+
+pub mod compress;
+pub mod decode;
+pub mod encode;
+
+use std::path::Path;
+
+use crate::engine::wal::checksum;
+use crate::error::Error;
+use crate::protocol::batch::Record;
+
+/// The four bytes at the start of every segment this build writes.
+pub const MAGIC: [u8; 4] = *b"LKLS";
+
+/// Version of the layout above. A file that says anything else is not this build's segment.
+pub const VERSION: u8 = 1;
+
+/// How many column streams a segment holds, in the fixed order [`encode::ORDER`] states.
+pub const COLUMNS: u8 = 3;
+
+/// The bytes a segment starts with: [`MAGIC`], [`VERSION`] and [`COLUMNS`].
+pub const HEADER_BYTES: [u8; 6] = [MAGIC[0], MAGIC[1], MAGIC[2], MAGIC[3], VERSION, COLUMNS];
+
+/// The length field in front of the column block: four bytes, little endian.
+pub const LENGTH: usize = 4;
+
+/// How many bytes of a segment come before its sealed block: the header and that length.
+pub const HEADER: usize = HEADER_BYTES.len() + LENGTH;
+
+/// The shortest thing that can be a segment: the header, an empty block and its seal.
+pub const MIN_BYTES: usize = HEADER + LENGTH + checksum::LEN;
+
+/// The bytes of one segment: every record it holds, encoded by columns and compressed.
+///
+/// The whole file is built here and written by [`write`], so that a caller can compare two segments
+/// or hand the bytes somewhere else without a filesystem in the way. Nothing here touches a disk.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Store`] when the compressor refuses — which for a block that fits in memory means the
+/// machine refused an allocation, not that the records were unusual.
+pub fn encode(records: &[Record]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error> {
+    let _ = records;
+    todo!("step lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments")
+}
+
+/// Read the records out of one segment's bytes.
+///
+/// The inverse of [`encode`], and the only reader: it is handed the bytes of a file this step wrote,
+/// a file somebody else wrote, a cut one or a corrupted one, and every one of those is either
+/// records or a typed error naming what failed.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Protocol`] when the bytes are not this build's segment: a header that does not say so, a
+/// block whose seal does not verify, a block that does not decompress to the length the header
+/// promised, or a column block that is not the layout [`encode`] writes.
+pub fn decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<Record>, Error> {
+    let _ = bytes;
+    todo!("step lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments")
+}
+
+/// Write `records` into `path` as one segment, replacing whatever is there.
+///
+/// A segment is written whole: the empty file an interrupted write would leave is not a segment, and
+/// [`decode`] refuses it instead of reading it as a store with no records in it. The directory is
+/// the caller's business — this call does not create it.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Store`] when the file cannot be written or flushed.
+pub fn write(path: &Path, records: &[Record]) -> Result<(), Error> {
+    let _ = (path, records);
